       (SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE).   This  policy  is  currently  implemented using GEDF
       (Global Earliest Deadline First)  in  conjunction  with  CBS  (Constant
       Bandwidth Server).  To set and fetch this policy and associated attrib‐
       utes,   one   must   use   the   Linux‐specific   sscchheedd__sseettaattttrr(2)  and
       sscchheedd__ggeettaattttrr(2) system calls.

       A sporadic task is one that has a sequence of jobs, where each  job  is
       activated  at most once per period.  Each job also has a _r_e_l_a_t_i_v_e _d_e_a_d_‐
       _l_i_n_e, before which it should finish execution, and a _c_o_m_p_u_t_a_t_i_o_n  _t_i_m_e,
       which is the CPU time necessary for executing the job.  The moment when
       a  task wakes up because a new job has to be executed is called the _a_r_‐
       _r_i_v_a_l _t_i_m_e (also referred to as the request time or release time).  The
       _s_t_a_r_t _t_i_m_e is the time at which a task starts its execution.   The  _a_b_‐
       _s_o_l_u_t_e _d_e_a_d_l_i_n_e is thus obtained by adding the relative deadline to the
       arrival time.

